// Classic P2PKH (Pay-to-Public-Key-Hash)
//
// This is the most common Bitcoin script pattern.
// The locking script checks that the spender provides:
//   1. A public key that hashes to the expected address
//   2. A valid signature from that key
//
// Initial stack (bottom to top):
//   <signature>
//   <pubkey>
//
// Simulated mode skips the signature check only. The hash check is real, so
// the pubkey must hash160 to the hash below. This stack fails at equalVerify:
//   0xdeadbeef 0xcafebabe
// To pass, replace the hash below with the hash160 of the pubkey you supply.
//
// The script flow:
//   [sig pubkey] → dup → [sig pubkey pubkey]
//                → hash160 → [sig pubkey hash]
//                → <expected_hash> → [sig pubkey hash expected]
//                → equalVerify → [sig pubkey]
//                → checkSig → [result]
//
// Toggle "Hex" view to see the compiled output matches the
// standard P2PKH template: 76 a9 14 <20-byte-hash> 88 ac

// Duplicate the public key for hashing
dup

// Hash the public key: RIPEMD160(SHA256(pubkey))
hash160

// Push the expected pubkey hash (20 bytes / 40 hex chars)
// Replace with your actual address hash
0x89abcdefabbaabbaabbaabbaabbaabbaabbaabba

// Verify the hashes match (fails if not equal)
equalVerify

// Verify the signature against the public key
checkSig
